The Tale of the Workers in the Vineyards
The kingdom of heaven is like a man who had a vineyard. He went out early in the morning to hire workers for his vineyard.
He agreed to pay them the usual wage for a day's work. Then he sent them into his vineyard.
At nine in the morning, he went out again. He saw people standing in the market doing nothing.
He told them, "You too, go help me in my vineyards. I will pay you what is fair."
He left. He came out again at about one o'clock and at three o'clock, doing just what he had done before.
Around five o'clock he went out and found other people standing. She asked them: "Why have you been standing here all day long without doing anything?"
They answered: 'Because no one has given us any work.' He said to them: 'You yourselves, go out into my vineyard and work.'
When evening came, the discussion took place between the generous host and the supervisor of the workers. He said to him: "Call the workers and pay them. Start with those who were hired last. And then pay those who were hired first."
The newly hired workers arrived around five o'clock. Each of them received the normal day's wage.
When the first employees came back, they expected to get more. But each one of them got the regular day's pay.
And when their messages reached him, he began to doubt his companion.
They said: "These people who were hired last only worked for one hour, and now they're getting the same as us. We're the ones who did most of the work and were under the sun all day."
The master of the house answered one of them. He said to him: "My good sir, I am being fair with you. Have you not agreed to work for the day's wage?"
God, your head, your money and walk. I wanted to give those I brought in the end the same salary I gave you.
