All the people from the country of Judea went to him. Even the people from Jerusalem went out to him. And they confessed their sins, and John baptized them in the river Jordan.
Juan wore a garment made of camel hair. He had a leather belt around his waist. And he ate locusts and wild honey.
This is what John used to say. "There will be people who come after me who are stronger than me. I am not worthy of bending down and untying his shoes for him."
"We baptize you with water. But he will baptize you with the Holy Spirit." The baptism and temptation of Jesus
In those days, John the Baptist came preaching in the wilderness of Judea, saying, "Repent, for the kingdom of heaven is at hand." This is he who was spoken of by the prophet Isaiah, saying, "The voice of one crying in the wilderness: 'Prepare the way of the Lord, make his paths straight.'" And the word of the Lord came to John the son of Zechariah, in the wilderness, and he came into all the country around the Jordan, preaching the baptism of repentance for the forgiveness of sins. And all the country of Judea and all the people of Jerusalem went out to him, and they were baptized by him in the river Jordan, confessing their sins. And he was clothed with camel's hair and a leather belt around his waist, and his food was locusts and wild honey. And he preached, saying, "After me, one is coming who is greater than I am, and I am not worthy to carry his sandals. I baptize you with water, but he will baptize you with the Holy Spirit." Now John himself was not baptizing, but his disciples were. And he testified, saying, "I am not the Christ." And they asked him, "Then who are you? Are you Elijah?" He said, "I am not." "Are you the Prophet?" He answered, "No." And they said to him, "Who are you, so that we may give an answer to those who sent us? What do you say about yourself?" And John answered them, saying, "I baptize you with water, but he who comes after me is stronger than I am, and his sandals I am not worthy to carry. He will baptize you with the Holy Spirit and fire. His winnowing fan is in his hand, and he will thoroughly clear his threshing floor and gather his wheat into the barn, but the chaff he will burn with unquenchable fire." And many people came out to him from Judea and all the region around the Jordan and Jerusalem and Bethany beyond the Jordan, where John was baptizing. And people were baptized by him in the river Jordan, confessing their sins. And the Pharisees and Sadducees came to John to be baptized by him. And they asked him, "Rabbi, what are you doing? Why are you baptizing people if you are not the Christ, nor Elijah, nor the Prophet?" And John answered them, "I baptize you with water, but one who is more powerful than I am is coming after me. I am not worthy to carry his sandals. He will baptize you with the Holy Spirit and fire. His winnowing fan is in his hand, and he will thoroughly clear his threshing floor and gather his wheat into the barn, but the chaff he will burn with unquenchable fire." After John had said these things, one of the Pharisees asked him to have lunch with him. So he went in and reclined at the table. And the Pharisee who had invited him saw him eating, not washing his hands before eating. (For the Pharisees and all the Jews do not eat unless they wash their hands, holding to the tradition of the elders. And when they come from the market, they do not eat unless they wash. And they hold to many other traditions: the washing of cups, pots, and bronze kettles; the lighting of lamps; the trimming of wicks; the setting of tables; and the clearing of tables. All these things the Pharisees do, but they do not do it. And as the Pharisee was saying these things, Jesus spoke up, "Now listen to what I, a humble fisherman, have to say about all this." And Jesus began to speak, "The scribes and the Pharisees sit in the chair of Moses. So you must be careful to do everything they tell you to do. But do not do what they do. For they
Jesus was coming up out of the water. And just then he saw heaven open and the Spirit descending on him like a dove.
He heard a voice from heaven saying to him, "You are my son, whom I love. I am very pleased with you."
Immediately the Spirit drove Jesus into the wilderness.
He was in the desert for forty days. Here, he was tempted by the devil. The beasts did not harm Jesus. Angels were protecting him. Jesus chose his first disciples.
And after John had been imprisoned, Jesus entered Galilee. He was proclaiming the gospel of God.
He said, "The time has come," "The kingdom of God is near. Turn away from your sins and believe the good news!"
One day, Jesus was walking by the Sea of Galilee. He saw Simon and his brother Andrew. They were casting a net into the sea. They were fishermen.
Come, follow me, said Jesus. "I will make you fishers of men."
Immediately, they left their nets and followed him.
Jesus walked a little further and saw James, the son of Zebedee, and his brother John. They were in the boat mending their nets.
Jesus quickly called them. They left his father Zebedi in the boat with his brothers. And they followed him. Jesus cast out a demon
Jesus and those who were with him went to Capernaum. When the Sabbath came, he entered the synagogue. He began to teach there.
People wanted someone like him, with his educational background. He used to explain things like someone with authority. He didn't talk like a teacher.
Suddenly, a man cried out in their temple. He was possessed by an evil spirit. And he said,
"What do you want from us, Jesus of Nazareth? Have you come to destroy us? I know who you are. You are the holy one of God!"
Jesus said firmly, "Be quiet!" "Come out of him!"
The evil spirit violently shook the man. Then it came out of him with a scream.
All the people were astonished at him. And they said to each other in amazement, "What is this? A new teaching! With authority! Even the demons are submitting to him!"
The news of Jesus spread quickly throughout Galilee. Jesus healed many people.
Jesus and those with him went out of the mosque. Immediately they went along with James and John to the house of Simon and Andrew.
Simon's mother-in-law was seriously ill. She had a fever. They were told about her condition.
He walked over to her. He took her hand and helped her stand up. The fever had left her. After that, she began to work for them.
That night, after sunset, people came to him in the wilderness, hungry. Large crowds followed him; those who were ill were also there. People brought to him even those who were possessed by demons.
All the people of the country gathered at the door.
Jesus healed many of them. They had all kinds of diseases. And he cast out many demons. But he did not allow them to speak, because they knew who he was. Jesus prayed in a quiet place.
It was very early in the morning and still dark. Jesus got up and went out of the house. He went to a place where he could be alone. There he prayed.
Simon and his companions went to look for Jesus.
When they found him, they cried out, "Everyone is looking for you!"
Jesus answered, "Let us go somewhere else. I want to go to the nearby villages. I must preach there because that is why I came."
That's why he traveled throughout the regions of Galicia. He used to preach in their mosques. And he used to expel jinn. Jesus healed a man who had a skin disease.
A man with a skin disease came to Si Issa. They made their bowings, and he humbly requested him. He said to him, "If you want to heal me and make my skin clean, you will be able to do it."
Jesus was terribly frightened. He stretched out his hand and touched the man. "I am ready to do it," he said. "Be 'pure'!"
Quickly, the illness left him. He recovered.
Jesus prayed for him earnestly. He warned him sternly.
He said, "Don't tell anyone about this," "Go to the priest and let him see you. Make the sacrifices that Moses commanded. This will be proof for the priest and for the people that you are 'pure'."
But the man went out and began to speak openly. He spread the news to all the people. This is what caused Jesus not to be able to enter any city openly. He stayed outside in empty places. But the people were coming to him from all around.
Jesus heals a man who cannot walk.
A few days later, Jesus entered Capernaum again. When the people heard that he had returned home,
A large crowd gathered until there was no more room for them to enter. Even outside the door, there was no more room for them to stand. And Jesus preached to them the Word.
Four people came holding a man who could not walk.
But they couldn't get it to Mr. Issa because of the crowd. Because they made a hole in the ceiling above Mr. Issa. Then they lowered him into the man through him onto a mat.
Jesus saw their faith. He said to the man, "Son, your sins are forgiven."
There were some law teachers. They were thinking,
Why is this man speaking in this way? He's saying something very bad! Only God will forgive sins!
Immediately Jesus perceived in His spirit what they were reasoning in their hearts. And He said to them, "Why are you reasoning about these things in your hearts?"
Which is easier to tell this man, "Your sins will be forgiven" or to tell him, "Stand up, take your mat and walk"?
I want you to know that the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins." And then, Jesus said to the man who could not walk:
He said to him, "As for your advice to me, get up. Take your mat and go to your house."
The man stood up and took his mat. He went out, looking at everyone. All the people there were amazed. They praised God and said, "We have never seen anything like this in our lives!" Jesus chose Levi.
Again Jesus went out beside the Sea of Galilee. A great crowd came to him. And he began to teach them.
As he was walking, he saw Levi, the son of Alphaeus. Levi was sitting at the tax office. Jesus said to him, "Follow me." Levi stood up and followed him.
Many people were following Jesus. One day, Jesus was having dinner at the house of a leper named Levi. Many tax collectors and other sinners were eating with Jesus and his disciples. These people were part of the large crowd that was following Jesus.
Some doctors of the law who were Pharisees saw Jesus eating with "sinners" and tax collectors. And they asked his disciples: "Why does he eat with tax collectors and sinners?"
Jesus heard that. He said to them, "The sick don't need a doctor, the healthy do. I didn't come to call those who think they are righteous before God, I came to call sinners to follow me."
John's disciples and the Pharisees used to fast. The disciples of Jesus came to him and said, "John's disciples fast, and the disciples of the Pharisees fast, but your disciples do not fast. Why is this?"
Jesus answered, "How can the guests of the bridegroom fast while he is with them? They cannot fast until he leaves."
But the time will come when the bridegroom will be taken from them. On that day they will fast from food.
Nobody makes a patch of new cloth for an old garment. That's why it just looks tight. This will only widen the hole.
People do not put new wine into old wineskins. Because the wine will burst the wineskins. And both the wine and the wineskins will be ruined. No, everyone puts new wine into new wineskins. Jesus is Lord of the Sabbath.
On the Sabbath, Jesus was walking with His disciples through the grain fields. The disciples began to pick some heads of wheat.
The Pharisees said to Jesus, "Look, your disciples are doing something that is not lawful to do on the Sabbath day."
Answer: "Have you not seen what David did? He and his companions were hungry and wanted to eat."
That was the time when Appear, the High Priest. David entered the house of God and ate the holy bread. Only the priests were allowed to eat. David gave some of it to his friends.
Jesus said to them, "The Sabbath was made to serve man, not man to serve the Sabbath."
Well, that man, the master, even on a Saturday.
Healing on Saturday
Once again Jesus entered the mosque. He was gathering men around him, men with one hand deformed and crooked.
Some Pharisees wanted to find fault with Jesus. They saw him as good. They wanted to see if he would heal this man on the Sabbath day.
Jesus said to the man who had the withered and crooked hand, "Come forward among the people."
Jesus asked the people: "What does the law say about what we must do on the Sabbath? Should we do good or evil? Should we help people and heal them, or should we kill them?" But no one gave an answer.
Jesus looked around at them with anger. He was very angry because their hearts were hardened. Then He said to the man, "Stretch out your hand." He stretched it out, and his hand was restored as good as new.
And then the Pharisees went and began to plot with the Herodians. They wanted to kill Jesus. The people were following Jesus.
Jesus went to the Sea of Galilee with his disciples. Great crowds followed him from the region of Galilee.
The people heard about everything Jesus was doing. Large numbers of people came to him. They came from Judea, Jerusalem, and Idumea. They came from the regions east of the Jordan River. And they came from the regions around Tyre and Sidon.
Because of the crowd, Jesus instructed his disciples to prepare a small boat for him. This way, the people would not crowd around him.
Jesus was healing many people. Because those who were sick were paying to touch him.
He is someone the people possessed by devils see, until they fall down before him. And the devils scream: "You, son of God!"
But our master Jesus ordered them not to reveal who he is. Our master Jesus appointed the twelve disciples.
Jesus went up a mountain. He called some people to come to him, and they came.
He appointed twelve of them and named them apostles. From then on, they were with him. He also sent them out to preach.
They will have the authority to cast out demons.
Jesus appointed the twelve. Simon was one of them. Jesus gave him the name Peter.
James, the son of Zebedee, and his brother John were called Boanerges by Jesus. Boanerges means Sons of Thunder.
There was Andrew, Philip, Bartholomew, Matthew, Thomas, and James the son of Alphaeus. And there was Thaddaeus and Simon the Zealot.
Judas Iscariot was one of them too. He is the one who will betray Jesus to his enemies. Jesus and Beelzebub
Jesus entered a house. The crowd gathered again. It was so large that Jesus and his disciples couldn't even eat.
His family heard about this. That's why they went to get him. They said, "This man is crazy."
Some doctors of the law were present. They had come from Jerusalem. They said, "This man is controlled by Beelzebul! By the ruler of demons, he is driving out demons."
Jesus called his disciples and said to them in parables: "How can Satan cast out Satan?"
If a country wages war on itself, it will not be able to stand.
If a family falls apart, it will not be able to stand.
